WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.900
Key management in C++ starts with treating keys as managed sensitive assets, not incidental strings.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.160
Every key should have a purpose, owner, access rule, storage location, and lifecycle.

3
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.960
Generate keys through approved sources and store them outside code in controlled backends.

4
00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:26.020
Avoid hardcoded secrets, developer convenience copies, and production material shared into lower environments.

5
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:30.140
Grant C++ processes only the key access they need.

6
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:38.880
Prefer handles or opaque references where possible, keep key-handling APIs narrow, and separate business logic from raw key material.

7
00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:46.200
Use keys through approved libraries, check return values, reject failed verification, and avoid accepting unauthenticated data.

8
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:50.060
A failed crypto operation is a security-relevant state.

9
00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.460
Plan rotation and revocation before production.

10
00:00:53.560 --> 00:01:02.360
Version keys, document compatibility windows, and define how old data, signatures, caches, and unavailable keys are handled.

11
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:09.520
Finally, keep secrets out of logs, crash artifacts, broad APIs, and long-lived memory where practical.

12
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:16.500
Review key boundaries, runtime configuration, and lifecycle behavior as security-critical engineering work.

13
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.900
The overall habit is to make key decisions visible.

14
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:30.080
If a reviewer can see purpose, ownership, storage, access, use, rotation, and failure behavior, the system is much easier to defend and maintain.

15
00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.160
Keys deserve the same engineering care as the data and identities they protect.
